1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:16,621
MATTHEW: Concord, New Hampshire. June 1816. A new governor takes office, and within weeks the state does something that sounds radical even now. It rewrites the charter of its elite private college and puts it under public control.

2
00:00:16,921 --> 00:00:27,571
MATTHEW: Dartmouth College becomes, by law, Dartmouth University. State-appointed trustees. A state board of overseers. The new university moves into the college buildings in Hanover.

3
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:33,676
WINNIE HOLLIS: Big Green, greetings from Hanover! And yes, for a while there were two Dartmouths in one little town.

4
00:00:33,976 --> 00:00:39,896
GARY MERCER: Two governors in Rhode Island, two Dartmouths in New Hampshire. You people really like doubles.

5
00:00:40,196 --> 00:00:44,307
MATTHEW: The governor in that chair was New Hampshire's number seven. William Plumer.

6
00:00:44,607 --> 00:00:55,413
MATTHEW: William Plumer was born June 25th, 1759, in Newburyport, Massachusetts. His family moved to Epping, New Hampshire, in 1768, when he was eight or nine. He did not go to college.

7
00:00:55,713 --> 00:01:03,681
MATTHEW: Before he was a lawyer, he was a Baptist lay preacher. Then he studied law and went into politics. He never sat in a college classroom. Later, he went after one.

8
00:01:03,981 --> 00:01:10,088
WINNIE HOLLIS: A preacher, a lawyer, then a governor. Very North Country. You do whatever the season needs.

9
00:01:10,388 --> 00:01:17,613
MATTHEW: He was Speaker of the New Hampshire House in 1791 and 1797. In 1802 he went to the United States Senate as a Federalist.

10
00:01:17,913 --> 00:01:27,808
MATTHEW: In 1803, when Jefferson bought Louisiana, some New England Federalists talked about leaving the Union. Plumer was one of them. Years later he called it the greatest political error of his life.

11
00:01:28,108 --> 00:01:31,809
GARY MERCER: Okay, a guy who admits he was wrong. I like him already.

12
00:01:32,109 --> 00:01:52,363
MATTHEW: Now, the count. From 1784 to 1792 New Hampshire's chief executive was called President, not Governor. The standard list counts those presidents anyway. Meshech Weare, John Langdon, John Sullivan, Josiah Bartlett. Then John Taylor Gilman, Jeremiah Smith. And seven, Plumer.

13
00:01:52,663 --> 00:02:06,620
MATTHEW: New Hampshire gives each person just one number, so Plumer's two runs, 1812 to 1813 and 1816 to 1819, both count as seven. Count only from the 1792 title of governor, and seven becomes Levi Woodbury.

14
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:10,113
GARY MERCER: And John Sullivan up there is the brother of the Massachusetts guy?

15
00:02:10,413 --> 00:02:16,211
MATTHEW: Same family, Gary. James was Massachusetts number seven. His brother John was New Hampshire number three.

16
00:02:16,511 --> 00:02:29,851
MATTHEW: So, Dartmouth. When Plumer came back in 1816, the college was in a feud between its president, John Wheelock, and its trustees. The state stepped in. The 1816 law reinstated Wheelock, added trustees, and gave the governor power over appointments.

17
00:02:30,151 --> 00:02:41,678
MATTHEW: The college's old trustees sued the state-approved secretary of the new board, William H. Woodward. The case went to the United States Supreme Court. Daniel Webster, Dartmouth class of 1801, argued for the college.

18
00:02:41,978 --> 00:02:52,194
MATTHEW: In 1819, Chief Justice John Marshall ruled for the college. A charter, he said, is a contract, and the Constitution forbids a state from breaking a contract. Dartmouth University disappeared.

19
00:02:52,494 --> 00:02:59,218
WINNIE HOLLIS: Stay green. Though honestly, the state wanted the college to answer to the public. That's not a crazy idea.

20
00:02:59,518 --> 00:03:12,800
MATTHEW: And here's the twist. That ruling reached far beyond Hanover. It told private corporations that their charters were contracts the states could not easily touch. States later learned to write in the right to change charters, but the case set the terms for generations.

21
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:26,515
MATTHEW: The case is called Trustees of Dartmouth College versus Woodward. And Webster's argument is remembered for one line, recorded later: It is, sir, as I have said, a small college. And yet there are those who love it.

22
00:03:26,815 --> 00:03:31,462
WINNIE HOLLIS: We put that line on everything, Gary. Mugs. Sweaters. Probably a canoe.

23
00:03:31,762 --> 00:03:34,154
GARY MERCER: So the little college beat the whole state.

24
00:03:34,454 --> 00:03:44,731
MATTHEW: The little college beat the whole state, and corporations everywhere got a shield. That's not a small thing, Gary. That's the kind of ruling bank lawyers and railroad lawyers would cite for the next century.

25
00:03:45,031 --> 00:03:59,301
MATTHEW: And look at what the college was chartered to do. Dartmouth's 1769 charter names the education of youth of the Indian tribes as a purpose. A Mohegan minister, Samson Occom, raised more than twelve thousand pounds in Britain to fund that mission.

26
00:03:59,601 --> 00:04:15,316
MATTHEW: Occom crossed to Britain with Nathaniel Whitaker in 1766. He preached across Britain from February 1766 to July 1767, raising money from congregations that came to see a Native minister in person. Wheelock had promised the money for a school for Native students.

27
00:04:15,616 --> 00:04:28,516
MATTHEW: Dartmouth's royal charter came on December 13th, 1769, from New Hampshire's colonial governor, John Wentworth, in the name of King George the Third. The charter's purposes put the Indian tribes first. The student body didn't.

28
00:04:28,816 --> 00:04:35,213
WINNIE HOLLIS: Hanover in the snow is beautiful. But the man who raised the money deserved a better school than the one he got.

29
00:04:35,513 --> 00:04:48,163
MATTHEW: Eleazar Wheelock used that money to found Dartmouth. And in its first two hundred years, nineteen Native American students graduated. It wasn't until 1970, under President John Kemeny, that Dartmouth officially recommitted to its founding mission.

30
00:04:48,463 --> 00:04:54,054
GARY MERCER: Twelve thousand pounds raised by a Native preacher, and nineteen graduates in two hundred years.

31
00:04:54,354 --> 00:05:00,028
MATTHEW: He lost. The trustees won. And the people the college was chartered to serve were mostly left outside.

32
00:05:00,328 --> 00:05:08,971
MATTHEW: In 1820, Plumer was a presidential elector. Every other elector in the country voted for James Monroe. Plumer alone voted for John Quincy Adams.

33
00:05:09,271 --> 00:05:18,937
MATTHEW: In that same 1820 vote, Plumer also declined to vote for the vice president, Daniel Tompkins. One elector, alone against the whole country, twice in one ballot.

34
00:05:19,237 --> 00:05:32,150
MATTHEW: His son, William Plumer Junior, went to Congress from 1819 to 1825. Plumer was elected to the American Antiquarian Society in 1815. He spent his old age writing, collecting, and keeping records of the men he'd served with.

35
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:43,533
GARY MERCER: Okay. Preacher, senator, secession guy, sorry about the secession, takes on a college, loses, votes alone, writes history till ninety-one. That's a full life.

36
00:05:43,833 --> 00:05:47,474
WINNIE HOLLIS: That's New Hampshire, Gary. Live free and argue with everybody.

37
00:05:47,774 --> 00:06:00,357
MATTHEW: He became the first president of the New Hampshire Historical Society. He died on December 22nd, 1850, at ninety-one. At the time he was the oldest living former governor in the country, and the oldest governor America had ever had.

38
00:06:00,657 --> 00:06:04,544
WINNIE HOLLIS: The lone dissenter. A very New Hampshire thing to be. Hollis out.

39
00:06:04,844 --> 00:06:08,072
MATTHEW: He lost. The trustees won. Ask who that ruling is still protecting.

40
00:06:08,372 --> 00:06:11,240
MATTHEW: So, New Hampshire. Your lucky seven, one more time.

41
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:15,534
MATTHEW: One. A Baptist lay preacher before he became a lawyer. No college.

42
00:06:15,834 --> 00:06:20,814
MATTHEW: Two. Speaker of the New Hampshire House, 1791 and 1797.

43
00:06:21,114 --> 00:06:25,657
MATTHEW: Three. He joined the 1803 secession talk, and later called it his greatest error.

44
00:06:25,957 --> 00:06:29,587
MATTHEW: Four. Counting the early presidents, Plumer is New Hampshire's 7th governor.

45
00:06:29,887 --> 00:06:33,781
MATTHEW: Five. Dartmouth College v. Woodward, 1819. The state lost.

46
00:06:34,081 --> 00:06:38,917
MATTHEW: Six. In Dartmouth's first 200 years, 19 Native American students graduated.

47
00:06:39,217 --> 00:06:44,811
MATTHEW: Seven. The lone 1820 elector against Monroe. Died at 91, the oldest governor ever.

48
00:06:45,111 --> 00:06:47,002
MATTHEW: New Hampshire. Keep it real.
